.TH GARNISH\-NETWORK.CONF 5 2026-11-04 garnish "garnish manual"
.SH NAME
garnish\-network.conf \- networks of garnish NAT VMs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/garnish/network.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR gerant\-net (8)
reads this file each time it creates a tap device for a VM with
.BR Mode=nat .
It sets the networks of every user's VMs. It is optional; without it, the
defaults below apply. It has to belong to root and not be writable by
anyone else, since the helper runs setuid root. The syntax is that of
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5).
.SH [NAT] SECTION
.TP
.BI Subnet= network
An IPv4 network from /16 to /30, split into /30 networks, one per running
VM. The
.IR n -th
uses tap
.BI garnish n\fR;\fP
the host has its first address and the guest its second. Pick one that
doesn't overlap a network the host is on. Default: 192.168.10.0/24, so the
first VM is 192.168.10.2, the second 192.168.10.6, and so on.
.TP
.BI Subnet6= network
An IPv6 network split into /64 networks, the
.IR n -th
for the same VM as the
.IR n -th
/30: the host is
.B ::1
in it and the guest
.BR ::2 .
It needs a /64 for each /30 (/58 or larger for a /24). Empty: IPv4 only.
Default: fd67:6172:6e69::/48.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
[NAT]
Subnet=10.123.0.0/24
Subnet6=
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gerant\-net (8),
.BR gerantd (1),
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5)
